summary.value:
This submission proposes a prior-based filtering criterion using corpus-level token frequency statistics (document-level mean/std of token priors) as a model-free, extremely efficient alternative to perplexity-based data pruning. 

Reviewers’ main concerns centered on the following aspects:

(i) whether the observed gains are convincing at the reported experimental scale (mostly ≤1.5B models and ~6B–12B token durations) and whether conclusions would persist at 7B–70B / 100B-token regimes; 

(ii) limited baseline breadth beyond PPL and DSIR (e.g., other lightweight heuristics/classifier-based filters), and sensitivity to hyperparameters such as filtering thresholds/ratios; 

(iii) potential over-filtering of rare but high-value content (minority languages, niche domains, symbolic data), and the degree to which token-frequency proxies can capture semantic coherence compared to PPL; 

(iv) clarity/positioning, with some reviewers viewing parts of the linguistic grounding and “learnability” claims as insufficiently quantified or potentially post-hoc.

In rebuttal, the authors addressed a substantial portion of these concerns by adding stronger comparisons, providing threshold/tokenizer ablations, and expanding scale via longer token-duration training and additional experiments on a larger 3B architecture (Qwen2.5-3B) where prior-based filtering remains consistently better than PPL across the reported benchmark suite. They also provided additional multilingual (e.g., Turkish) and rare-terminology robustness evidence, and clarified the intended scope/limitations of the method (structure/noise detection vs semantic nonsense where PPL may help). The primary remaining concern is the lack of direct validation at truly large model/data scales and the inherent limitation that frequency-based proxies may not fully capture semantic coherence; however, given the strong, reproducible empirical evidence across many tasks, the significant efficiency benefit, and the strengthened rebuttal, I consider the work a solid, practically impactful contribution with clear limitations to be stated in the final version.
